|@@background-color:#0d6efd;color:#fff;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:3px;@@ Active|Normal processing states in the pipeline|
|@@background-color:#fd7e14;color:#fff;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:3px;@@ Gate|Waiting or hand-off states requiring external action|
|@@background-color:#dc3545;color:#fff;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:3px;@@ Failure|Error or failed-delivery states|
|@@background-color:#198754;color:#fff;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:3px;@@ Terminal|Final states that end the lifecycle|

---

!! Syntax

```
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> StateA
    StateA --> StateB : event label
    StateB --> [*]

    state "Display Label" as StateA

    state CompoundState {
        [*] --> SubA
        SubA --> SubB
    }

    classDef myStyle fill:#color,stroke:#color,color:#fff
    class StateA myStyle
```

'' `[*]` '' is the implicit start and end pseudo-state. Use it on both ends of terminal states.

!! Tips

* Use `stateDiagram-v2` (not the legacy `stateDiagram`).
* Avoid `:` inside state identifiers or labels — colons are the transition-label delimiter and will break the parser.
* Use `state "Long Label" as ShortId` when labels contain spaces or special characters.
* `classDef` applies to states the same way it does for flowchart nodes; pair with a legend for clarity.
* Compound states (nested `state { ... }`) model concurrent or hierarchical state machines.
* See also: [[Flowchart]] for process flows, [[Sequence Diagram]] for actor interactions.

